temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task were satisfied with direct functional evidence: SSH remains open to any address, TCP/9000 is accepted only from node2 and rejected from node3 and other sources via permanent firewalld rich rules, and the configuration was proven persistent through an actual reboot of node1 with matching pre/post rule sets and re-verified accept/reject behavior. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also completed and evidenced on all three nodes with clear boot-identity transitions and recovered state, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only notable shortcoming is minor hygiene residue: a test socat listener and associated log files left running/behind on node1 (and stray reboot logs on node2/node3) after verification, which were not cleaned up.","limitations":["The reject rule was only functionally tested from node3, not from a broad sample of 'everywhere else'; however, since the rule matches TCP/9000 from any source at priority 100 (after the specific node2 accept), this generalizes with high confidence.","A test socat listener on port 9000 and its log file were left running on node1 after verification rather than being torn down, and reboot/test log files were left in /tmp on all three nodes — bounded, low-risk residue but not cleaned up."]},"ttft_ms":22639,"type":"result","duration_ms":71262,"uuid":"7f76dd91-9acd-49f6-bc16-e0ca408670ac","ttft_stream_ms":2441,"time_to_request_ms":38,"queued_turn_count":0}
